## POST 77: Best Siding for Florida: Hurricane-Rated Options

# Best Siding for Florida: Hurricane-Rated Options

**Meta Title:** Hurricane-Rated Siding for Florida | Wind-Resistant Options **Meta Description:** Florida Building Code siding requirements, wind ratings, impact resistance, moisture, heat tolerance, and insurance discounts for coastal homes.

**Primary Keyword:** siding installation florida (30/mo, KD 0) **Secondary Keywords:** impact resistant siding, wind rated siding, hurricane siding, florida building code siding, coastal siding options

Living in Florida means preparing for hurricane season. Your home’s siding is your first line of defense against wind, impact, and moisture intrusion. Florida Building Code requires specific performance standards, and selecting hurricane-rated siding offers protection, compliance, and often insurance benefits.

## Florida Building Code Siding Requirements

### Wind Rating Requirements

Florida’s building code mandates siding capable of withstanding specific wind speeds depending on location:

**Coastal High Hazard Areas (typically within 1 mile of coast):** - Wind speeds up to 130+ mph - High-velocity hurricane zone (HVHZ) standards apply - Impact-rated materials required

**Non-Coastal Urban Areas (Tampa, etc.):** - Wind speeds 110–130 mph - Standard impact resistance required - Most quality siding materials qualify

**Rural Areas:** - Wind speeds 90–110 mph - Standard siding materials acceptable

### Code Compliance Labels

Look for siding certified to: - **ASTM D3161:** Vinyl siding standards - **ASTM A792:** Aluminum siding standards - **ASTM C1186:** Fiber cement standards - **Florida Product Approval (FPA):** State-specific certification - **Miami-Dade or Broward approval:** Highest coastal standards

Contractors should verify compliance for your specific area.

## Material Performance in Hurricane Conditions

### Impact-Rated Vinyl Siding

**Wind Rating:** Up to 130 mph **Impact Rating:** Resists 9-pound sphere impact at 50 mph **Features:** - Thicker, reinforced formulation - Enhanced UV protection (reduces fading) - Insulated backing improves thermal efficiency - Color options comparable to standard vinyl

**Cost:** $15–$25 per linear foot (vs $12–$20 standard) **Lifespan in Florida:** 20–30 years **Best for:** Non-coastal Tampa, Pasco, Polk areas

**Pros:** - Affordable impact-resistant option - Low maintenance - Good insulation - Widely available

**Cons:** - Not suitable for HVHZ coastal zones - Can still crack in severe impacts - Fades faster than premium materials

### Aluminum Siding (Premium Grade)

**Wind Rating:** Up to 140 mph **Impact Rating:** Excellent resistance to dents and cracks **Features:** - Heavier gauge (.040“–.050” thickness) - Enhanced corrosion protection (important for salt spray) - Excellent durability in humid climate

**Cost:** $18–$28 per linear foot **Lifespan in Florida:** 30–50 years **Best for:** Coastal areas, salt spray zones

**Pros:** - Superior corrosion resistance - Excellent wind and impact performance - Long lifespan - Low maintenance

**Cons:** - More expensive than vinyl - Can dent from severe impacts - Professional repair needed - Minimal insulation value

### Fiber Cement Siding

**Wind Rating:** Up to 140 mph+ **Impact Rating:** Excellent (cement-based material) **Features:** - Naturally impact-resistant - Minimal color fading - Superior moisture management - Can mimic wood or modern aesthetic

**Cost:** $22–$32 per linear foot **Lifespan in Florida:** 25–40 years **Best for:** Premium installations, coastal areas, long-term value

**Pros:** - Highest durability and impact resistance - Minimal fading in Florida sun - Excellent moisture management (important for humidity) - Can be stained or painted - Most insurance-favorable material

**Cons:** - Most expensive option - Requires periodic sealing/repainting - Professional installation essential - Heavy (requires experienced installers) - Higher maintenance than vinyl/aluminum

## Specific Coastal Considerations

### Salt Spray and Corrosion

Homes within 1 mile of coast face accelerated corrosion from salt-laden air.

**Best materials for salt spray:** 1. **Fiber cement:** Naturally impervious to corrosion 2. **High-grade aluminum:** With advanced protective coating 3. **Premium impact vinyl:** With UV-stabilized formulation

**Avoid in coastal areas:** - Standard aluminum (corrodes quickly) - Wood siding (rapid deterioration) - Standard vinyl (short lifespan)

### Moisture and Humidity Management

Florida’s 70%+ humidity requires siding that manages moisture without trapping it.

**Best moisture management:** - Fiber cement: Breathable, sheds moisture effectively - Aluminum: Non-porous, prevents water retention - Impact vinyl: Good moisture barrier when properly installed

**Critical:** Proper underlayment and ventilation are as important as material choice.

## Insurance Benefits for Hurricane-Rated Siding

Many Florida insurers offer discounts for hurricane-rated siding installations:

**Typical discounts:** 5–15% on homeowner’s insurance premiums **Payback period:** Can offset higher material cost in 3–5 years **Additional benefits:** May reduce claim deductibles or improve claim settlement amounts

**To qualify for discounts:** - Install FPA or Miami-Dade-approved material - Use licensed contractor - Obtain certification documentation - Coordinate with insurer before installation

## Wind Rating vs. Impact Rating: What’s the Difference?

**Wind Rating:** How well siding resists pressure from sustained high winds (measured in mph)

**Impact Rating:** How well siding resists damage from flying debris or direct impacts (measured by sphere impact tests)

Florida’s HVHZ areas require *both* high wind ratings *and* impact ratings. Standard siding has wind ratings but fails impact tests.

## Best Siding for Different Florida Regions

### Tampa Area (Non-Coastal)

- **Recommended:** Impact-rated vinyl or aluminum

- **Premium option:** Fiber cement

- **Wind zone:** 110–130 mph

- **Impact rating:** Required

### Coastal Tampa, Clearwater, St. Petersburg

- **Recommended:** Premium aluminum or fiber cement

- **Avoid:** Standard vinyl

- **Wind zone:** 130+ mph (HVHZ in some areas)

- **Impact rating:** High impact rating essential

- **Salt spray:** Corrosion protection critical

### Pasco and Polk Counties (Inland)

- **Recommended:** Impact-rated vinyl or aluminum

- **Wind zone:** 100–120 mph

- **Impact rating:** Preferred

## FAQs About Hurricane-Rated Siding

**Q: Is impact-rated siding necessary in non-coastal Tampa?** A: Florida Building Code requires it. Beyond code, it improves resilience and may qualify for insurance discounts.

**Q: Which siding material performs best in hurricanes?** A: Fiber cement and premium aluminum perform best. Impact-rated vinyl is a good affordable option for non-coastal areas.

**Q: Do I need HVHZ-certified siding?** A: Only if you live in a designated HVHZ zone (typically 1 mile of coast). Ask your contractor or building department.

**Q: Will hurricane-rated siding lower my insurance?** A: Many insurers offer 5–15% discounts. Verify with your agent before installation.

**Q: How do I know if siding is truly hurricane-rated?** A: Look for FPA (Florida Product Approval), Miami-Dade, or Broward certification labels. Contractors should provide documentation.

## Protect Your Home with the Right Siding

Florida hurricanes don’t discriminate between homes; proper siding selection and installation protect your investment and your family.

**JR One Aluminum** specializes in Florida Building Code-compliant installations and carries certified, impact-resistant siding materials suitable for all Tampa-area zones. We’ll help you select the right material for your location and budget.

**Call (844) 444-3114 for a free consultation about hurricane-rated siding options.** We’ll explain code requirements for your area, discuss material options, and help you qualify for insurance discounts.

